\chapter{User Documentation}
\label{ch:user}

This chapter provides comprehensive documentation for users of the Nebula Power platform. It is structured to address the needs of different user roles, including system administrators, technical support staff, and end customers. The documentation covers installation procedures, user interfaces, operational workflows, and troubleshooting guidance.

\section{System Overview}
\label{sec:user-overview}

The Nebula Power platform enables monitoring and management of solar energy installations, with particular focus on payment compliance and system security. This section provides a high-level overview of the system from a user perspective.

\subsection{Key Components}
\label{subsec:user-components}

From a user perspective, the Nebula Power platform consists of several interconnected components:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Web Dashboard}: A responsive web application that provides access to monitoring data, payment information, and system settings.
    \item \textbf{Mobile Application}: A companion mobile app that offers on-the-go access to essential system features.
    \item \textbf{On-site Hardware}: Physical devices installed at solar installation sites, including inverters, meters, and communication modules.
    \item \textbf{Notification System}: Channels for alerts and notifications, including email, SMS, and in-app messaging.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: System components diagram showing relationship between hardware and software elements]}}
    \caption{Key components of the Nebula Power platform from a user perspective}
    \label{fig:user-components}
\end{figure}

\subsection{User Roles}
\label{subsec:user-roles}

The Nebula Power platform supports multiple user roles, each with specific permissions and access levels:

\begin{description}
    \item[System Administrator] Manages system configuration, user accounts, and global settings. Has full access to all system features and data.
    \item[Customer Support] Handles customer inquiries, monitors payment status, and manages basic customer account operations.
    \item[Field Technician] Responsible for installation, maintenance, and troubleshooting of on-site hardware.
    \item[Financial Officer] Manages payment plans, processes transactions, and generates financial reports.
    \item[Security Officer] Monitors security alerts, investigates tampering incidents, and manages security policies.
    \item[Customer] End-user who has a solar installation managed by the system. Has access to their own installation data, payment information, and basic settings.
\end{description}

Access privileges are assigned based on the principle of least privilege, ensuring users have access only to the functions and data necessary for their role.

\begin{table}[h]
    \centering
    \begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|}
        \hline
        \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Admin} & \textbf{Support} & \textbf{Technician} & \textbf{Finance} & \textbf{Customer} \\
        \hline
        User Management & Full & Limited & No & No & Self Only \\
        \hline
        Energy Monitoring & Full & View & View & View & Own Only \\
        \hline
        Payment Processing & Full & View & No & Full & Own Only \\
        \hline
        Security Alerts & Full & View & View & No & Limited \\
        \hline
        System Configuration & Full & No & Limited & No & No \\
        \hline
        Reports & Full & Limited & Limited & Full & Limited \\
        \hline
    \end{tabular}
    \caption{Access privileges by user role}
    \label{tab:role-privileges}
\end{table}

\section{Getting Started}
\label{sec:getting-started}

This section guides users through the initial setup and access procedures for the Nebula Power platform.

\subsection{System Requirements}
\label{subsec:system-requirements}

\subsubsection{Web Dashboard}
The web dashboard is accessible through modern web browsers and requires:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Supported Browsers}: Google Chrome (v88+), Mozilla Firefox (v85+), Microsoft Edge (v88+), Safari (v14+)
    \item \textbf{Internet Connection}: Minimum 1 Mbps download speed
    \item \textbf{Screen Resolution}: Minimum 1280 × 768 pixels
    \item \textbf{JavaScript}: Enabled
    \item \textbf{Cookies}: Enabled for authentication and preferences
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Mobile Application}
The mobile application is available for iOS and Android platforms:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{iOS}: iPhone 6S or newer running iOS 13.0+
    \item \textbf{Android}: Devices running Android 8.0 (Oreo) or newer
    \item \textbf{Storage}: Minimum 100 MB free space
    \item \textbf{Permissions}: Camera (for QR code scanning), Push Notifications, Location (optional)
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{On-site Hardware}
The monitoring hardware requires:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Power Supply}: Stable power connection or battery backup
    \item \textbf{Network Connectivity}: Cellular (3G/4G), Wi-Fi, or wired Ethernet
    \item \textbf{Operating Environment}: Temperature range of -10°C to 55°C, humidity up to 95\% non-condensing
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Account Setup}
\label{subsec:account-setup}

\subsubsection{Registration Process}
New users can register for the Nebula Power platform through the following steps:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Navigate to the registration page at \url{https://nebula-power.com/register} or through the mobile app.
    \item Enter required information:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Email address (will be used as username)
        \item Password (minimum 8 characters, including uppercase, lowercase, number, and special character)
        \item Full name
        \item Phone number
        \item Organization name (if applicable)
    \end{itemize}
    \item Accept the terms of service and privacy policy.
    \item Complete the email verification process by clicking the link sent to the registered email address.
    \item Set up multi-factor authentication (optional but recommended).
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Screenshot of the registration form]}}
    \caption{User registration interface}
    \label{fig:registration}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Login Procedure}
Registered users can access the system through:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Navigate to the login page at \url{https://nebula-power.com/login} or launch the mobile app.
    \item Enter email address and password.
    \item Complete multi-factor authentication if enabled.
    \item For administrators and support staff, select the organization context if managing multiple organizations.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Screenshot of the login page]}}
    \caption{User login interface}
    \label{fig:login}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Password Management}
Users can manage their passwords through the following functions:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Password Reset}: Available through the "Forgot Password" link on the login page. Requires access to the registered email address.
    \item \textbf{Password Change}: Available in the user profile settings. Requires current password verification.
    \item \textbf{Password Expiry}: System administrators can configure password expiry policies requiring periodic password changes.
\end{itemize}

\section{User Interface Guide}
\label{sec:ui-guide}

This section provides a detailed overview of the Nebula Power platform's user interface, highlighting key screens and navigation patterns.

\subsection{Dashboard}
\label{subsec:dashboard}

The dashboard is the primary landing page after login, providing an at-a-glance overview of the system status and key metrics.

\subsubsection{Administrator Dashboard}
The administrator dashboard includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{System Health Summary}: Status of all monitored installations and services
    \item \textbf{Recent Alerts}: List of recent security and system alerts requiring attention
    \item \textbf{Customer Status}: Overview of customer compliance and payment status
    \item \textbf{Energy Production Summary}: Aggregated production data across all installations
    \item \textbf{Quick Actions}: Shortcuts to common administrative tasks
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Screenshot of the administrator dashboard]}}
    \caption{Administrator dashboard with system overview}
    \label{fig:admin-dashboard}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Customer Dashboard}
The customer dashboard includes:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Installation Status}: Current status of the customer's solar installation
    \item \textbf{Energy Production}: Real-time and historical energy production data
    \item \textbf{Energy Consumption}: Energy usage patterns and analytics
    \item \textbf{Payment Status}: Current payment status and upcoming payment information
    \item \textbf{Alerts and Notifications}: Recent system alerts related to the customer's installation
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Screenshot of the customer dashboard]}}
    \caption{Customer dashboard showing installation performance and payment status}
    \label{fig:customer-dashboard}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Energy Monitoring Interface}
\label{subsec:energy-interface}

The energy monitoring interface provides detailed insights into energy production and consumption patterns.

\subsubsection{Real-time Monitoring}
The real-time monitoring view displays:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Current Power Generation}: Live power generation in watts or kilowatts
    \item \textbf{Current Power Consumption}: Live power consumption in watts or kilowatts
    \item \textbf{Battery Status}: Current charge level and charging/discharging status (if applicable)
    \item \textbf{System Efficiency}: Real-time efficiency metrics
    \item \textbf{Weather Conditions}: Current weather data affecting energy production
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Real-time energy monitoring interface]}}
    \caption{Real-time energy monitoring display}
    \label{fig:realtime-energy}
\end{figure}

\subsubsection{Historical Data Analysis}
The historical data view enables users to:

\begin{itemize}
    \item Select different time periods (day, week, month, year, custom range)
    \item Compare data across multiple time periods
    \item View energy production and consumption trends
    \item Analyze performance metrics over time
    \item Export data in various formats (CSV, PDF)
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[h]
    \centering
    \framebox{\parbox{0.8\textwidth}{\centering [Figure: Historical energy data visualization]}}
